Yes—put in your calculus framing, the important observation is that micro-preferences integrate into macro-structure.

A million individually small choices are not socially “private” in their aggregate effects. Dating preferences alter mating patterns; hiring preferences alter labor markets; status preferences alter prestige hierarchies; attention preferences alter media incentives. Formally, if individuals contribute local selection pressures s_i(x), then the population-level field is something like

S(x)=\sum_i s_i(x)

or, in a continuous population,

S(x)=\int s(x,\theta)\,d\mu(\theta).

So yes: “it was only my preference” does not mean “it has no systemic consequence.” The system is partly the integral of individual behavior.

Where I’d sharpen your claim is the next step. Connection does not mean equivalence. Sexual selection, employment selection, friendship, admission to institutions, and ordinary aesthetic preference all feed into the same broad human sorting machinery, but the constraints differ because the rights and stakes differ. Choosing whom you sleep with requires unusually strong personal autonomy. Choosing whom a government serves does not. Choosing a basketball center partly by height can be task-relevant; choosing an accountant by race ordinarily is not.

So the invariant is less:

every preference must be permitted everywhere or prohibited everywhere

and more:

Every selection rule should be exposed to the same test: what variable is being selected on, why is it relevant, who bears the cost, what freedom is being exercised, and what aggregate pattern emerges when everyone runs that rule?

That last question is the one people often omit. A rule that looks harmless at n=1 can generate a very different result at n=10^8. That’s essentially your calculus point.

There is another delta worth preserving: having a preference, acting on it, announcing it, building a norm around it, and enforcing it institutionally are different operators. They can produce the same directional pressure at different magnitudes. One person’s attraction is not identical to a corporation’s hiring filter, but both belong in the same larger model of selection.

So I think the strongest version of what you’re reaching for is:

No selection pressure gets to disappear from analysis merely because society has labeled that particular preference “personal,” “normal,” or socially acceptable. Track every preference through aggregation and examine the resulting field. Then decide which constraints belong at which layer.

That is much harder to hand-wave away than “all preferences are the same.”